<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1040">1040</a>–1105</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Shlomo Yitzchaki, Shlomo Yitzhaki, Jarchi, Yarhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rashi, Shlomo Yitzchaki, was a French rabbi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> who wrote comprehensive commentaries on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and is generally considered a leading biblical exegete of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. He studied in Worms under Yaakov ben Yakar and Isaac ben Eliezer Halevi, then returned to Troyes, where he joined and later led the rabbinical court and around 1070 founded a yeshiva. His commentary on nearly all of the Babylonian Talmud has been printed in every edition since Daniel Bomberg's of the 1520s, and his commentary on the Five Books of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> is the basis of more than 300 supercommentaries. He mourned the victims of the People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1096 in penitential poems, and died in 1105.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rashi</b> of Troyes, in the 11th century, wrote the most widely used commentary on the Bible and Talmud; Moses Maimonides, in 12th-century Cairo, wrote the Mishneh Torah, a code of all Jewish law, and the Guide for the Perplexed, a work of philosophy; Joseph Karo’s Shulchan Aruch of…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars such as <b>Rashi</b>, in 11th-century France, wrote commentaries on the Bible and Talmud still studied today, and Maimonides wrote his great code of Jewish law, the Mishneh Torah, in clear Hebrew in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Kept to few occupations, many lent money at interest, which the Church forbade Christians to do; others traded, and the commentary of <b>Rashi</b> of Troyes (1040–1105) is printed in every edition of the Talmud.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li></ul></section>
